    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "Using updated satellite and oceanographic data, this paper links recent increases in Arctic freshwater flux to a decline in Labrador Sea deep water formation, suggesting a direct weakening effect on the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRACE",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MOUNTED_ON",
            "target_entity_instance": "satellite",
            "target_entity_type": "Platform",
            "paragraph": "Here we derive a new estimate of the recent freshwater flux from Greenland using updated GRACE satellite data, present new flux estimates for heat and salt from the North Atlantic into the Labrador Sea and explain recent variations in LSW formation.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GRACE",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "ice mass loss",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "Numerous studies have described recent acceleration of Greenland's ice mass loss6\u201310. We use GRACE data updated to October 2014 to derive a new acceleration estimate and its onset time (Methods).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is an important component of ocean thermohaline circulation. Melting of Greenland's ice sheet is freshening the North Atlantic; however, whether the augmented freshwater flux is disrupting the AMOC is unclear.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Labrador Sea Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Labrador Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The Labrador Sea is a key location for the formation of one of the dense, deep-water components of the AMOC via winter convection; however, the process is sensitive to surface conditions21. Wood et al.5 suggest the possibility of a shutdown in Labrador Sea convection in response to global warming. Kuhlbrodt et al.22 provide a theoretical stability analysis, and suggest that winter convection in the Labrador Sea can be turned off by increased freshwater input.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Irminger Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Labrador Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Northward reduction in heat and salt transport between the Cape Desolation and Paamiut sections likely reflects strong offshore eddy transport32, advecting Irminger Water into the interior of the Labrador Sea.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Irminger Current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Greenland",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Myers et al.33,48 showed that a significant fraction of freshwater originating in and around Greenland is transported to the Labrador Sea: melt water from eastern Greenland is entrained in the East Greenland Current, where it moves south and merges with the Irminger Current as it rounds Cape Farewell; melt water from southwestern Greenland joins the West Greenland Current, similarly merging with the Irminger Current (Fig. 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
